@@ -0,0 +1,98 @@
+cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.25)
+
+project(ItchOrderBook)
+
+set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)
+set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED True)
+
+list(APPEND CMAKE_MODULE_PATH ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/cmake)
+
+include(FetchContent)
+
+# Add Abseil
+FetchContent_Declare(
+  abseil
+  GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/abseil/abseil-cpp.git
+  GIT_TAG        20240116.0
+)
+set(ABSL_PROPAGATE_CXX_STD ON)
+set(ABSL_ENABLE_INSTALL OFF)
+FetchContent_MakeAvailable(abseil)
+
+# Fetch the bgen library
+FetchContent_Declare(
+  bgen
+  GIT_REPOSITORY https://github.com/tidwall/bgen.git
+  GIT_TAG main
+)
+FetchContent_MakeAvailable(bgen)
+
+find_package(fmt REQUIRED)
+find_package(flatbuffers_interface REQUIRED)
+
+# Add external hash map libraries
+set(EXTERNAL_LIBRARIES_DIR ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/external)
+
+add_executable(itch_order_book
+    src/main.cpp
+    src/bufferedreader.cpp
+)
+
+# Link against the FlatBuffers interface library from fb_schema
+target_link_libraries(itch_order_book 
+    PRIVATE 
+        flatbuffers_interface
+        fmt::fmt
+        absl::flat_hash_map
+)
+
+# Include directories for all the hash map implementations
+target_include_directories(itch_order_book PRIVATE
+    ${EXTERNAL_LIBRARIES_DIR}/flat_hash_map
+    ${EXTERNAL_LIBRARIES_DIR}/parallel-hashmap
+    ${bgen_SOURCE_DIR}
+)
+
+# Add compiler flags from run.sh
+target_compile_options(itch_order_book PRIVATE
+    -g
+    -O3
+    -march=native
+    -Wall
+    -Wextra
+    -Wswitch-enum
+)
+
+# Optional: Improve error reporting consistency with run.sh
+if(CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID STREQUAL "Clang" OR CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER_ID STREQUAL "AppleClang")
+    target_compile_options(itch_order_book PRIVATE -ferror-limit=2)
+endif() 
+
+# Add load_fbs executable
+add_executable(load_fbs
+    src/load_fbs.cpp
+)
+
+# Link against the same libraries as itch_order_book, plus unordered_dense
+target_link_libraries(load_fbs 
+    PRIVATE 
+        flatbuffers_interface
+        fmt::fmt
+        absl::flat_hash_map
+)
+
+# Include same libraries for load_fbs
+target_include_directories(load_fbs PRIVATE
+    ${EXTERNAL_LIBRARIES_DIR}/flat_hash_map
+    ${EXTERNAL_LIBRARIES_DIR}/parallel-hashmap
+)
+
+# Add the same compiler flags
+target_compile_options(load_fbs PRIVATE
+    -g
+    -O3
+    -march=native
+    -Wall
+    -Wextra
+    -Wswitch-enum
+)
diff --git a/itch-order-book/README_L2_vs_L3.md b/itch-order-book/README_L2_vs_L3.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c7c03ce
--- /dev/null
+++ b/itch-order-book/README_L2_vs_L3.md
@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+# L2 vs L3 Order Book Performance Comparison
+
+This document compares the performance difference between L2 (price level aggregated) and L3 (individual order tracking) order book implementations.
+
+## Implementation Details
+
+The order book implementation in `order_book.h` has been modified to support both L2 and L3 modes via compile-time configuration. This is controlled by the `kOrderBookConfig::TRACK_ORDERS_IN_LEVEL` setting:
+
+```cpp
+// In kOrderBookConfig struct
+static constexpr bool TRACK_ORDERS_IN_LEVEL = true;  // true for L3, false for L2
+```
+
+When `TRACK_ORDERS_IN_LEVEL` is set to `true`, the order book maintains a collection of individual orders at each price level, allowing for precise order-by-order tracking and time priority (FIFO) queue management. This is a full L3 order book.
+
+When set to `false`, the order book only tracks the aggregate quantity at each price level without maintaining individual order details. This is an L2 order book.
+
+Instead of using preprocessor macros, the implementation now uses C++17's `if constexpr` to conditionally compile only the code needed for each mode:
+
+```cpp
+void add_order(order_id_t oid, qty_t qty, timestamp_t timestamp = timestamp_t(0)) {
+  if constexpr (kOrderBookConfig::TRACK_ORDERS_IN_LEVEL) {
+    level_order_entry_t entry{qty, timestamp, oid, false};
+    m_orders.push_back(entry);
+  }
+}
+```
+
+This approach allows the compiler to eliminate the code paths that aren't needed based on the configuration setting, while keeping all the code in a more readable form.
+
+## Performance Improvements
+
+### Evolution of L3 Performance
+
+We've made several implementation changes to improve L3 performance:
+
+1. **Original (Map-based) Implementation**:
+   - Used `phmap::btree_map<order_id_t, level_order_entry_t>` to store orders at each price level
+   - Provided ordered iteration and O(log n) lookup
+   - Performance: 321.03 ns/packet
+
+2. **Vector-based Implementation**:
+   - Replaced map with `std::vector<level_order_entry_t>` to store orders
+   - Provides better cache locality, reduced memory allocation overhead
+   - Linear search for order lookups, but typically fast for small collections
+   - Performance: 297.79 ns/packet (7.2% improvement)
+
+3. **Lazy Deletion Implementation**:
+   - Added a `deleted` flag to `level_order_entry_t` instead of immediately removing entries
+   - Defers actual removal until a cleanup threshold is reached (25% deleted entries)
+   - Uses erase-remove idiom during cleanup to efficiently batch removals
+   - Reduces vector resizing and element shifting operations
+   - Performance: 305.25 ns/packet (slight regression from vector-only approach)
+
+4. **Vector Preallocation Implementation**:
+   - Maintains the vector and lazy deletion approach
+   - Preallocates vector capacity (reserves space for 8 orders initially)
+   - Reduces the number of reallocations and copies during order additions
+   - Performance: 286.71 ns/packet (10.7% improvement over the original implementation)
+
+### Current Performance Results
+
+The performance was tested with 50 million packets from NASDAQ ITCH feed data.
+
+| Mode | Implementation | Processing Time (ns) | Average Time per Packet (ns) | vs L2 |
+|------|---------------|----------------------|------------------------------|-------|
+| L2   | N/A           | 10,128,820,084      | 202.58                       | 0%    |
+| L3   | btree_map     | 16,051,680,422      | 321.03                       | +58%  |
+| L3   | vector        | 14,889,491,750      | 297.79                       | +47%  |
+| L3   | vector + lazy | 15,262,567,196      | 305.25                       | +51%  |
+| L3   | prealloc vector | 14,335,510,541    | 286.71                       | +41%  |
+
+**Performance Comparison**:
+- The L3 order book with preallocated vector storage is approximately **41% slower** than the L2 order book.
+- The best L3 implementation (preallocated vector) is **10.7% faster** than the original map-based L3 implementation.
+- Preallocation provides a **3.7% improvement** over the simple vector approach and **6.1% improvement** over lazy deletion.
+
+## Trade-offs
+
+### L3 Order Book (Full Order Tracking)
+- **Pros**:
+  - Maintains full order-by-order information
+  - Supports time priority (FIFO) queue management
+  - Can replay exact order execution sequence
+  - Essential for some trading strategies requiring precise queue position
+  
+- **Cons**:
+  - Higher memory usage
+  - Lower performance (41% slower than L2 in our best implementation)
+  - More complex implementation
+  - Linear search for order modifications and cancellations
+
+### L2 Order Book (Level Aggregation)
+- **Pros**:
+  - Faster processing (41% improvement over best L3 in our tests)
+  - Lower memory usage
+  - Simpler implementation
+  
+- **Cons**:
+  - No individual order tracking
+  - Cannot determine queue position or time priority
+  - Unsuitable for strategies requiring exact order sequence
+
+## Optimization Insights
+
+Our testing revealed several interesting performance characteristics:
+
+1. **Data Structure Selection**: The choice between tree-based maps and contiguous arrays (vectors) has a significant impact. For our workload, vectors provided better performance than btree maps despite theoretical O(n) vs O(log n) lookup complexity.
+
+2. **Lazy Deletion Trade-offs**: While lazy deletion theoretically reduces vector modifications, the additional overhead of checking the deletion flag and performing periodic cleanups outweighed the benefits in our specific workload. This demonstrates the importance of measuring actual performance rather than relying purely on algorithmic complexity analysis.
+
+3. **Memory Allocation Matters**: Preallocating vector capacity proved to be the most effective optimization, reducing the need for frequent reallocation during high-frequency order addition. This highlights how memory allocation overhead can be a significant factor in performance-critical applications.
+
+4. **Memory Access Patterns**: The vector-based approach likely benefits from better cache locality, as elements are stored contiguously in memory. This highlights how modern CPU architecture considerations can sometimes trump theoretical algorithm analysis.
+
+## Conclusion
+
+The choice between L2 and L3 depends on your specific requirements:
+
+- For high-frequency trading where microseconds matter and full order details aren't required, L2 provides significantly better performance.
+- For strategies requiring precise queue position or order sequence information, L3 is necessary despite the performance cost.
+
+Among the L3 implementations, the preallocated vector approach provided the best overall performance for our test dataset, improving the original map-based implementation by 10.7%.
+
+Our implementation allows switching between these modes at compile time, making it easy to benchmark and choose the appropriate mode for your specific needs.
+
+## Implementation Note
+
+The implementation uses a C++ configuration struct (`kOrderBookConfig`) with `constexpr` variables to control features like order tracking:
+
+```cpp
+// To switch to L2 mode:
+static constexpr bool TRACK_ORDERS_IN_LEVEL = false;
+
+// To switch to L3 mode:
+static constexpr bool TRACK_ORDERS_IN_LEVEL = true;
+
+// Vector preallocation size:
+static constexpr size_t LEVEL_ORDERS_INITIAL_CAPACITY = 8;
+```
+
+With C++17's `if constexpr`, the compiler can optimize out unused code at compile time, providing the performance benefits of preprocessing directives while maintaining cleaner, more maintainable code. 
